# TEMPLATE — the R2 token CNPG uses to archive keycloak-db.
#
# A sibling r2-backup-credentials.enc.yaml already exists and is genuinely
# sops-encrypted. Its header still says "PLACEHOLDER — NOT ENCRYPTED YET", which
# is STALE: `sops -e -i` encrypts values and leaves comments alone, so the
# instruction survived the act of following it. The ciphertext lengths (32 and
# 64 bytes) match real R2 key material, not the word "PLACEHOLDER".
#
# It still needs replacing, for a different reason: that token was scoped to
# bucket `asp-cnpg-staging`, and the ObjectStore here now targets
# `cnpg-staging-keycloak` so this namespace cannot rewrite asp's archive. A
# bucket-scoped token for the old bucket cannot write to the new one.
#
#   1. R2 → create bucket `cnpg-staging-keycloak`.
#   2. R2 → API tokens → create a token with **Object Read & Write** on THAT
#      BUCKET ONLY. Not account-wide: the blast radius of this credential is the
#      only thing separating an identity backup from the asp archive.
#   3. Fill and encrypt:
#
#        cd infrastructure/services/staging/keycloak/database
#        cp r2-backup-credentials.enc.yaml.example r2-backup-credentials.enc.yaml
#        # paste the two values below, then:
#        sops --encrypt --in-place r2-backup-credentials.enc.yaml
#
#      The staging age key is selected by path (.sops.yaml, `staging/*.enc.yaml`).
#      Encrypting is not optional — an unencrypted match of that path pattern is
#      a live R2 token in git history.
#
#   4. Verify after Flux reconciles and the first ScheduledBackup runs:
#
#        kubectl -n identity get backup
#        kubectl -n identity exec keycloak-db-1 -c plugin-barman-cloud -- \
#          barman-cloud-backup-list --cloud-provider aws-s3 \
#          s3://cnpg-staging-keycloak keycloak-db
#
# Until a working token is in place the ScheduledBackup FAILS — visibly, in the
# Backup object's status. That matters more here than for the other clusters:
# MCP users are created in the admin console, so this archive is the only copy
# of who may read the fbref database.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: r2-backup-credentials
type: Opaque
stringData:
  ACCESS_KEY_ID: REPLACE_WITH_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID
  ACCESS_KEY_SECRET: REPLACE_WITH_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
